-- New-style daily words for April (days 91–120): a real devotion (paragraphs ending "Today: …"), a declaration and a prayer. Same KJV verse as before.
-- Only replaces days still in the old one-paragraph style, so anything rewritten in Admin → Devotions is kept.

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah wrote these words about seven hundred years before Jesus was born. He described a Servant of the Lord who would be despised and rejected, a man of sorrows, acquainted with grief. People would turn their faces away from Him. And then this verse: "But he was wounded for our transgressions, he was bruised for our iniquities: the chastisement of our peace was upon him; and with his stripes we are healed."

Read it slowly and notice the words "our" and "we". He was wounded for our wrongs. He was bruised for our sins. The punishment that brings us peace fell on Him.

When the early Christians read Isaiah 53, they saw the cross clearly. Philip explained this very chapter to an Ethiopian official riding in his chariot, and the man believed and was baptised that same day.

Jesus did not suffer for His own sins. He had none. He stood in our place. Every lash of the whip, every thorn, every nail was part of carrying what we deserved.

"With his stripes we are healed." Our deepest healing is the healing of our broken relationship with God. And from that healing flows hope for every other kind of brokenness.

Today: read Isaiah 53 slowly. Wherever you see "we", "our" or "us", say your own name instead, and thank Jesus for taking your place.',
  declaration = 'Jesus was wounded for my transgressions and bruised for my iniquities. His punishment brought me peace, and by His stripes I am healed.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for being wounded for me. You carried what I deserved. I receive Your peace and Your healing today. Let my life be a grateful response to Your love. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy091' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Peter was there when Jesus was arrested. He followed at a distance, and then denied three times that he even knew Him. He saw from afar what happened next. Years later, writing to suffering believers, he said: "Who his own self bare our sins in his own body on the tree, that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness: by whose stripes ye were healed."

"His own self": Jesus did not send someone else. He carried our sins personally.

"In his own body": the cost was physical and real.

"On the tree": Peter uses the word for wood, reminding his Jewish readers that the law said anyone hung on a tree was under a curse. Jesus took the curse for us.

Then Peter gives the purpose: "that we, being dead to sins, should live unto righteousness." The cross is not only about forgiveness of the past. It is about a new life now. Sin no longer has the right to rule us.

Peter knew this personally. After his denial, the risen Jesus restored him by a lakeside and gave him a new calling. The man who failed became a leader of the church.

Today: think of one sin or habit you have felt powerless against. Tell Jesus: "You carried this on the tree. I am dead to it. Help me live for what is right."',
  declaration = 'Jesus carried my sins in His own body on the tree, so I am dead to sin and alive to righteousness.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for carrying my sins in Your own body. Thank You for restoring Peter, and for restoring me. Break the power of the sins that still pull at me, and help me live for what is right. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy092' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Ephesians 2, Paul describes what we were without Christ: dead in sins, following the ways of the world, by nature children of wrath. Then comes the turning point: "But God". And a few verses later: "For by grace are ye saved through faith; and that not of yourselves: it is the gift of God: Not of works, lest any man should boast."

Grace means a gift we do not deserve. Salvation is not a salary we earn by being good, attending church, giving money or praying long prayers. It is a gift God gives freely.

Faith is how we receive it. Faith is not a work that earns the gift; it is the open hand that receives it.

"Not of works, lest any man should boast." Nobody in heaven will be able to say, "I got here because I was better than others." Everyone will say, "I am here because of Jesus."

This is good news for people who feel they are not good enough. It is also humbling for people who think they are.

Paul continues in verse 10: we are God''s workmanship, created in Christ Jesus for good works. We are not saved by good works, but we are saved for them. Good works are the fruit, not the root.

Today: stop trying to earn God''s love. Thank Him for the gift of salvation, and let your good works today flow from gratitude, not fear.',
  declaration = 'I am saved by grace through faith. It is God''s gift, not my work, and I live my life in grateful response.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for the gift of salvation that I could never earn. I receive it by faith. Free me from trying to prove myself to You. Let my good works today flow from gratitude for Your grace. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy093' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'On the night before Israel left Egypt, God told each family to kill a lamb without blemish and put its blood on the doorposts and lintel of their house. That night, judgment would come on Egypt. But God promised: "when I see the blood, I will pass over you, and the plague shall not be upon you to destroy you."

The safety of each family did not depend on how good they were, how strong their house was or how much they prayed that night. It depended on the blood.

That night became the Passover, a feast Israel celebrated every year to remember their deliverance. And it was during the Passover feast, centuries later, that Jesus ate His last supper with His disciples, took bread and wine, and spoke of His body and His blood.

John the Baptist had called Jesus "the Lamb of God, which taketh away the sin of the world." Paul wrote, "Christ our passover is sacrificed for us." The Passover lamb pointed to Jesus.

We are not saved by our own efforts, but by His blood. When we trust in Him, we are covered.

Today: thank God for the blood of Jesus that covers you. When fear or guilt rises, remember: God sees the blood, and judgment passes over.',
  declaration = 'I am covered by the blood of Jesus, the Lamb of God. When God sees the blood, judgment passes over me.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for the blood of Jesus, our Passover Lamb. I do not trust in my own goodness, but in His sacrifice. Cover me, my family and my home, and help me live in grateful freedom. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy094' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'After hours on the cross, after the darkness and the cry "My God, my God, why hast thou forsaken me?", Jesus received a little vinegar to drink. Then He spoke His final words recorded by John: "It is finished." He bowed His head and gave up His spirit.

In Greek, "It is finished" is one word: tetelestai. It was used in everyday life for completing a task or paying a debt in full. Jesus was not saying, "I am finished", as a defeated man. He was declaring, "It is accomplished."

What was finished? The work His Father gave Him to do. The sacrifice for sin. The payment of the debt we could never pay. Nothing needs to be added to it.

Many people live as though the cross was a good start, but they must finish the job with their own efforts. They try to pay God back, earn His favour or make up for their failures. But Jesus said it is finished.

Notice also that Jesus "gave up the ghost". No one took His life from Him by force. He laid it down willingly, just as He said He would.

This does not mean the Christian life involves no effort. It means our efforts are no longer about paying for sin. They are a response of love.

Today: if you have been trying to pay for your past, stop. Say aloud: "Jesus said, it is finished. My debt is paid."',
  declaration = 'Jesus said, "It is finished." My debt is paid in full. Nothing needs to be added to His work.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You that Your work on the cross is complete. I stop trying to pay for my sins. I rest in what You have finished. Let my life now be a response of love, not an attempt to earn. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy095' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hebrews 11 lists many heroes of faith. Then chapter 12 begins by picturing them as a great cloud of witnesses surrounding us, as if we are running a race in a stadium. And we are told how to run: lay aside every weight and sin, run with patience, and "Looking unto Jesus the author and finisher of our faith."

Runners know that where you look affects how you run. If you keep looking at the crowd, at other runners or at your own feet, you lose focus. You run best with your eyes on the finish line.

Jesus is both the author and the finisher of our faith. He started it, and He will complete it.

Then the writer tells us how Jesus faced the cross: "who for the joy that was set before him endured the cross, despising the shame." The cross was not joyful. It was agony and humiliation. But Jesus looked beyond it to the joy ahead: the joy of redeeming us, rising again and sitting at the right hand of God.

When life is hard, we need the same focus. We look at Jesus, who went through suffering and came out victorious. And we remember the joy set before us too.

Today: if you are discouraged or tired in your faith, fix your eyes on Jesus. Read a passage about His life and ask Him to help you run your race with patience.',
  declaration = 'I fix my eyes on Jesus, the author and finisher of my faith. He endured the cross, and He will help me finish my race.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I fix my eyes on You. Help me lay aside every weight and sin that slows me down. Give me patience to run my race. Thank You that You started my faith, and You will finish it. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy096' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote this to the Galatians, who were being told that faith in Christ was not enough and that they also needed to keep certain religious laws to be right with God. Paul answers with his own testimony: "I am crucified with Christ: nevertheless I live; yet not I, but Christ liveth in me: and the life which I now live in the flesh I live by the faith of the Son of God, who loved me, and gave himself for me."

When Jesus died, Paul says, the old Paul died with Him. The proud religious man trying to earn God''s approval was nailed to the cross. Yet Paul still lived, but now with a new source of life: Christ living in him.

The Christian life is not just about trying to copy Jesus from the outside. It is Jesus Himself living in us by His Spirit, changing us from the inside.

And look at how personal Paul''s words are: "who loved me, and gave himself for me." Not just "loved the world" in general, but "loved me".

You can say that too. Jesus loved you personally and gave Himself for you personally.

Today: say Galatians 2:20 aloud slowly, putting emphasis on "me". Then ask Christ to live His life through you in one specific situation you will face today.',
  declaration = 'I am crucified with Christ, yet I live. Christ lives in me, and I live by faith in the Son of God, who loved me and gave Himself for me.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You that You loved me and gave Yourself for me. My old life was crucified with You. Live Your life in me today, in my words, my choices and my relationships. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy097' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hosea spoke to a nation that had been wounded because of its sin. In this chapter, the people speak about returning to the Lord and say: "After two days will he revive us: in the third day he will raise us up, and we shall live in his sight."

In its first meaning, this was a hope that God would restore Israel quickly after judgment. But the phrase "the third day" became deeply meaningful to the early Christians. Jesus rose from the dead on the third day. Paul says Christ "rose again the third day according to the scriptures."

In Scripture, the third day often marks a turning point: Abraham saw the mountain where he would offer Isaac on the third day, and Esther went in to the king on the third day.

Many of us are living in "day two": the day after the loss, before the breakthrough. Everything feels finished. Hope seems buried. The disciples felt like that on the Saturday after the cross.

But God specialises in third days. He raises up what looks dead: dreams, marriages, faith, people.

"We shall live in his sight." The goal of resurrection is not just escape from trouble, but life in God''s presence.

Today: name your "day two" situation. Pray over it with hope, remembering that the God of the third day is able to raise it up.',
  declaration = 'The God of the third day revives me and raises me up. I will live in His sight.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You know the things in my life that feel dead and buried. I bring my "day two" to You. Revive me, raise me up and let me live in Your presence. I trust You, the God of the third day. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy098' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Very early on the first day of the week, women came to the tomb with spices to anoint Jesus'' body. They expected to find a corpse. Instead, there was an earthquake, the stone was rolled away, and an angel sat on it. The guards shook with fear.

The angel said to the women: "Fear not ye: for I know that ye seek Jesus, which was crucified. He is not here: for he is risen, as he said. Come, see the place where the Lord lay."

"As he said." Jesus had told His disciples many times that He would be killed and rise again on the third day. They did not understand. Now His words had come true.

"Come, see." The resurrection is not a myth or a feeling. The tomb was empty. The angel invited the women to look for themselves. And then Jesus Himself met them on the road.

This changes everything. If Jesus is risen, then His words are true. His sacrifice was accepted. Death is defeated. And He is alive today, not just a memory.

The women were sent to tell the disciples. The first witnesses of the resurrection were women, at a time when their testimony was often not taken seriously. God chose them anyway.

Today: tell someone today, in your own words, why you believe Jesus is alive and what difference it makes to you.',
  declaration = 'Jesus is not in the tomb. He is risen, as He said. His words are true, and He is alive today.',
  prayer = 'Risen Lord Jesus, thank You that the tomb is empty and You are alive. Thank You that Your words always come true. Give me courage to tell others that You are risen, and help me live in the power of Your resurrection. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy099' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Lazarus had been dead for four days when Jesus arrived in Bethany. His sister Martha came out to meet Him and said, "Lord, if thou hadst been here, my brother had not died." She believed in a resurrection one day, at the last day. But Jesus said to her: "I am the resurrection, and the life: he that believeth in me, though he were dead, yet shall he live."

Martha was thinking of resurrection as an event far in the future. Jesus told her that resurrection is a Person, and He was standing in front of her.

Then He asked her, "Believest thou this?" And Martha answered with one of the great confessions of faith: "Yea, Lord: I believe that thou art the Christ, the Son of God."

Soon after, Jesus wept at the tomb. He knew He was about to raise Lazarus, yet He still felt the pain of death and grief. Then He called, "Lazarus, come forth," and the dead man walked out.

For believers, death is not the end. Our bodies may die, but we will live, because Jesus is the resurrection and the life.

If you are grieving a loved one who trusted Jesus, this verse is for you. Jesus weeps with you, and He also promises they will live.

Today: hear Jesus asking you, "Do you believe this?" Answer Him honestly in prayer, and bring Him any area of your life where you need resurrection hope.',
  declaration = 'Jesus is the resurrection and the life. Because I believe in Him, even death cannot hold me.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You are the resurrection and the life. I believe in You. Thank You that You weep with us in our grief and give us hope beyond the grave. Bring Your life into the dead places of my heart and circumstances. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy100' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = '1 Corinthians 15 is the great resurrection chapter. Paul explains that Christ died for our sins, was buried and rose again on the third day. He lists people who saw the risen Jesus, including more than five hundred at once. Then he says that if Christ is not risen, our faith is useless. But Christ is risen.

Near the end, Paul shouts with joy: "O death, where is thy sting? O grave, where is thy victory? The sting of death is sin; and the strength of sin is the law. But thanks be to God, which giveth us the victory through our Lord Jesus Christ."

Think of a honeybee. Once it has left its sting in someone, it cannot sting anyone again. Death is like that for believers. Christ took its sting, sin, into Himself on the cross.

Death is still real. We still grieve. Funerals are still painful. But death no longer has the final word for those who belong to Jesus.

Notice that victory is a gift: "which giveth us the victory." We do not win it by our own strength. God gives it through Jesus.

Paul ends the chapter with a practical command: be steadfast, unmovable, always abounding in the work of the Lord, because your labour is not in vain.

Today: thank God for the victory over death in Jesus. Then do one act of service for the Lord, knowing it is not in vain.',
  declaration = 'Death has lost its sting. Thanks be to God, who gives me the victory through my Lord Jesus Christ.',
  prayer = 'Thank You, Father, for the victory over sin and death through Jesus. When I fear death or grieve, remind me that it has lost its sting. Make me steadfast and unmovable, abounding in Your work. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy101' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 6, Paul answers a question: if God''s grace is so great, should we keep sinning so that grace can increase? His answer is a strong "God forbid!" Then he explains what baptism means.

"Therefore we are buried with him by baptism into death: that like as Christ was raised up from the dead by the glory of the Father, even so we also should walk in newness of life."

Baptism is a picture. Going down into the water is like being buried with Christ. Coming up is like being raised with Him to a new life.

The point is not just a ceremony. It is a change of identity. The old self, ruled by sin, was buried. A new self, alive to God, was raised.

"Walk in newness of life" is practical. It means living differently in our homes, workplaces and relationships. Not perfectly, but genuinely new.

Many people treat their Christian life like an old car with a new paint job: the same engine under a shiny cover. God is not offering a new coat of paint. He is offering a new life, powered by the same Spirit who raised Jesus from the dead.

Today: if you have been baptised, remember that day and thank God for what it means. If you have not, ask God whether it is time to take that step of obedience. Then choose one way to walk in newness of life today.',
  declaration = 'I have been buried with Christ and raised with Him. I walk in newness of life.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that my old life was buried with Christ, and that I have been raised with Him. Help me walk in newness of life today, in my words, choices and relationships, by the power of Your Spirit. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy102' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Colossians, Paul was concerned that believers were being pulled toward rules about food, special days and spiritual experiences that made them look holy but did not change their hearts. In chapter 3 he points them to a better focus: "If ye then be risen with Christ, seek those things which are above, where Christ sitteth on the right hand of God."

If Jesus is risen and seated at God''s right hand, and we are raised with Him, then our lives have a new centre. Our hearts and minds should be directed toward Him.

"Seek those things which are above" does not mean ignoring earthly responsibilities. Paul goes on to talk about family life, work and relationships. It means seeing all of life in the light of Christ.

What we focus on shapes us. If our minds are full of envy, lust, anger or fear, our lives will reflect it. If our minds are full of Christ, His character will grow in us.

Think of a sunflower. It turns its face toward the sun throughout the day. In the same way, we are meant to keep turning our hearts toward Christ.

Today: notice what your thoughts keep returning to today. Whenever you notice your mind drifting to things that pull you down, gently turn it toward Christ: a verse, a prayer or a song.',
  declaration = 'I am raised with Christ, so I seek the things above, where Christ is seated at the right hand of God.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I am raised with You. Turn my heart and mind toward You. Free me from focusing on things that pull me down, and help me see my whole life in the light of Your victory. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy103' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Romans 7 ends with Paul''s honest struggle: "O wretched man that I am! who shall deliver me from the body of this death?" Then Romans 8 begins with one of the most freeing sentences ever written: "There is therefore now no condemnation to them which are in Christ Jesus."

"Now": not one day in heaven, but now.

"No condemnation": not a little, not reduced, but none.

Condemnation is the verdict of guilty and the sentence that follows. For those in Christ, that verdict has been removed, because Jesus took the condemnation for us on the cross.

This does not mean we never feel guilty, or that sin does not matter. The Holy Spirit still convicts us when we do wrong, to lead us back to God. But conviction and condemnation are different. Conviction says, "You did wrong; come back to God." Condemnation says, "You are worthless; stay away from God."

The rest of Romans 8 describes life in the Spirit: freedom, adoption as God''s children, hope and unbreakable love.

Many believers live under a cloud of condemnation, always feeling God is disappointed with them. That is not God''s voice.

Today: when you feel condemned, ask: "Is this the Spirit leading me to repent, or the accuser trying to push me away?" Confess any real sin, and then stand on this truth: there is now no condemnation for me in Christ.',
  declaration = 'There is now no condemnation for me, because I am in Christ Jesus.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that there is no condemnation for those in Christ. When I sin, help me listen to Your Spirit''s conviction and run back to You. Silence every voice of condemnation and help me live in Your freedom. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy104' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul was given "a thorn in the flesh". We do not know exactly what it was. He calls it "the messenger of Satan to buffet me". Three times he asked God to remove it. God''s answer was not what Paul wanted: "My grace is sufficient for thee: for my strength is made perfect in weakness."

God did not take the thorn away. He gave grace instead.

Paul''s response is surprising: "Most gladly therefore will I rather glory in my infirmities, that the power of Christ may rest upon me." He realised that his weakness had become a place where Christ''s power could be seen.

We often think God can only use us when we are strong, confident and problem-free. But many of God''s greatest works happen through weak people: Moses who said he could not speak well, Gideon who felt like the least in his family, Paul with his thorn.

This is not a promise that every hardship will stay. God does heal and deliver. But when He does not remove a thorn, His grace is enough to carry us through it.

Notice too that grace is not a one-time gift. "My grace is sufficient", present tense. Every day, every weakness, there is enough grace.

Today: name one weakness or ongoing struggle in your life. Tell God honestly how you feel about it. Then ask Him to show His strength in that very place.',
  declaration = 'God''s grace is sufficient for me. His strength is made perfect in my weakness.',
  prayer = 'Lord, You know the thorn I carry. I have asked You to take it away. Whether You remove it or not, let Your grace be enough for me. Let Your strength be seen in my weakness, and help me trust You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy105' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote to Titus, who was leading young churches on the island of Crete. The Cretans had a bad reputation, and the churches needed to learn how to live godly lives. Paul told Titus how older men, older women, young women, young men and servants should live. Then he gave the reason: "For the grace of God that bringeth salvation hath appeared to all men."

Grace "appeared". It showed up in history, in a Person. When Jesus came, God''s grace became visible.

And it appeared "to all men". Not only to the religious, the educated or the respectable. To everyone. No one is too far, too sinful or too ordinary.

Paul continues in the next verse: this grace teaches us to deny ungodliness and worldly lusts, and to live soberly, righteously and godly in this present world. Grace does not only save us. It trains us.

Some people think grace means God does not care how we live. But real grace is like a good teacher. It forgives our mistakes, but it also patiently teaches us to live differently.

Think of a child learning to write. A good teacher does not throw the child out for every mistake, but she also does not leave the child writing badly forever. She keeps correcting with kindness.

Today: thank God that His grace reached you. Then ask Him what one thing His grace is teaching you to change right now.',
  declaration = 'The grace of God has appeared to me. It saves me and teaches me to live a godly life.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You that Your grace appeared in Jesus and reached me. Thank You that You do not only forgive, but also teach me. Train me by Your grace to say no to ungodliness and yes to a life that honours You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy106' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'John wrote his first letter to believers who were confused about sin. Some claimed they had no sin at all. Others were weighed down by guilt. John answers both: "If we say that we have no sin, we deceive ourselves, and the truth is not in us." Then: "If we confess our sins, he is faithful and just to forgive us our sins, and to cleanse us from all unrighteousness."

To confess means to agree with God about our sin: to call it what He calls it, without excuses or blaming others.

Notice the two words describing God: "faithful and just". Faithful means He keeps His promise to forgive. Just means forgiveness is fair, because Jesus has already paid for our sin. God is not ignoring sin when He forgives us. He is applying the payment of the cross.

Then there are two actions: forgive and cleanse. Forgiveness removes the guilt. Cleansing deals with the stain and the inner effect of sin.

Some believers confess the same sin again and again, never believing they are forgiven. Others never confess at all. John invites us to a simple, honest rhythm: confess, receive forgiveness, be cleansed, walk on in the light.

Today: take a few minutes to ask the Holy Spirit to show you any sin you need to confess. Name it honestly, then thank God that He has forgiven you and cleansed you.',
  declaration = 'When I confess my sins, God is faithful and just to forgive me and cleanse me from all unrighteousness.',
  prayer = 'Father, I agree with You about my sin. I confess it without excuse. Thank You that You are faithful and just to forgive me, because Jesus paid for it. Cleanse me and help me walk in Your light. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy107' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 6, Paul explains what happens when someone belongs to Christ. Their old self was crucified with Him, so they are no longer slaves to sin. Then comes this promise: "For sin shall not have dominion over you: for ye are not under the law, but under grace."

"Dominion" means rule, control or lordship. Sin may still tempt us. We may still stumble. But sin is no longer our master.

Why? "for ye are not under the law, but under grace." The law shows us what is right, but it cannot give us the power to do it. It is like a mirror that shows the dirt on your face but cannot wash it. Grace is different. Grace forgives us and gives us a new heart and the Holy Spirit to live in a new way.

Many believers struggle with habits that seem to rule them: anger, lust, lying, addiction, bitterness. They try harder and harder by willpower, and they fail and feel hopeless.

Paul''s message is that freedom comes through grace, not through trying harder in our own strength. We receive grace, yield to God and walk in the Spirit, and sin''s grip begins to loosen.

Freedom may be a process. But the truth is settled: sin is not your master anymore.

Today: name one sin that has felt like a master. Pray: "In Christ, you do not rule me. I am under grace." Ask God for one practical step toward freedom.',
  declaration = 'Sin shall not have dominion over me, because I am not under law but under grace.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I bring You the sins that have felt like my master. Thank You that in Christ they no longer rule me. Fill me with Your grace and Your Spirit, and show me the next step toward freedom. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy108' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Peter wrote this letter to believers scattered across different regions. They were facing suffering and rejection. Yet he begins with praise: "Blessed be the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, which according to his abundant mercy hath begotten us again unto a lively hope by the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead."

"Begotten us again" means we were born again. A new birth gives us a new life and a new family.

"Unto a lively hope" means a living hope. Many hopes are dead or dying: hopes in money, politicians, relationships or our own strength. They fade or disappoint. But Christian hope is alive because it rests on a living Saviour.

The source is "abundant mercy". We did not earn this new life. It came from God''s great mercy.

And the foundation is "the resurrection of Jesus Christ from the dead". Because He lives, our hope lives.

Peter goes on to describe an inheritance that is incorruptible, undefiled and does not fade away, reserved in heaven for us. Earthly inheritances can be lost through bad management, family disputes or inflation. Ours is kept safe by God.

Today: if you feel hopeless about something, write it down. Then write underneath: "My hope is alive because Jesus is alive." Thank God for His abundant mercy and your living hope.',
  declaration = 'By God''s great mercy I have been born again to a living hope through the resurrection of Jesus Christ.',
  prayer = 'Blessed be You, God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ. Thank You for Your abundant mercy and for my new birth. When my hopes fade, remind me that my hope is alive because Jesus is alive. In His name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy109' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The women came to the tomb early in the morning with spices. They found the stone rolled away and the body of Jesus gone. While they stood there confused, two men in shining clothes appeared. The women were afraid and bowed their faces to the ground. Then the angels asked: "Why seek ye the living among the dead? He is not here, but is risen."

It is a gentle rebuke. The women were looking in the wrong place. They came to honour a dead teacher. But Jesus was alive.

Then the angels said, "remember how he spake unto you when he was yet in Galilee." Jesus had told them He would be crucified and rise on the third day. Grief had made them forget.

We sometimes look for life in dead places. We look for joy in things that cannot give it. We keep returning to old sins, old relationships or old ways of thinking, expecting them to satisfy us. We search for Jesus in empty religion instead of living relationship.

We also forget His words when we are afraid. That is why it is so important to remember what He has said.

Today: ask yourself, "Where am I looking for life among the dead?" Turn from that place, and look for Jesus where He is: alive, in His Word, in prayer and among His people.',
  declaration = 'I do not seek the living among the dead. Jesus is risen, and I remember His words.',
  prayer = 'Risen Lord, forgive me for looking for life in dead places. Help me remember Your words when I am afraid or grieving. Show me where You are working, and help me follow You as the living Lord. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy110' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote to the Colossians that in Christ we have been made complete. He explains how God forgave us: "Blotting out the handwriting of ordinances that was against us, which was contrary to us, and took it out of the way, nailing it to his cross."

"The handwriting" was a written record of debt, like a signed IOU. It listed what we owed God because of our sin.

God did two things with it. He blotted it out, wiping away the writing. Then He took it out of the way, nailing it to the cross.

In those days, when a person was crucified, the charge against them was often written and fixed to the cross. Above Jesus'' head was written, "The King of the Jews." But Paul says something else was nailed there too: our record of debt. Jesus took our charge sheet and paid it.

The next verse says that God also disarmed the spiritual powers and triumphed over them through the cross. The accuser has no valid document to hold against us anymore.

Many believers live as if their debt list is still active. They keep paying with guilt, fear and self-punishment. But the record has been cancelled.

Today: write down a few things you feel guilty about that you have already confessed. Then write across the page: "Nailed to the cross." Tear it up and thank God for paying your debt.',
  declaration = 'God has blotted out the record of debt against me and nailed it to the cross. My debt is cancelled.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for cancelling my record of debt through Jesus. Thank You that it is nailed to the cross. Help me stop living as if I still owe what He has paid. I receive Your forgiveness with gratitude. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy111' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'The apostle John was an old man, exiled on the island of Patmos for his faith. One Lord''s day he heard a loud voice like a trumpet and turned to see the risen Jesus in glory. His face shone like the sun, His eyes were like flames of fire, and His voice was like many waters. John fell at His feet as though dead.

Jesus laid His right hand on him and said: "Fear not; I am the first and the last: I am he that liveth, and was dead; and, behold, I am alive for evermore, Amen; and have the keys of hell and of death."

Jesus knows death from the inside. He was dead. But now He is alive forever.

He holds "the keys of hell and of death". In those days, keys meant authority. Whoever had the keys could open and close, release or lock. Jesus is saying death does not have the final say. He does.

This was comfort to persecuted believers. Some would be killed for their faith. But the One who held the keys was on their side.

It is comfort for us too. Whatever we fear: sickness, violence, accidents, our own death or the death of loved ones, Jesus holds the keys.

Today: if you are afraid of death or loss, picture the risen Jesus placing His hand on you and saying, "Fear not." Thank Him that He holds the keys.',
  declaration = 'Jesus was dead, but He is alive for evermore, and He holds the keys of death and hell. I will not fear.',
  prayer = 'Risen Lord Jesus, You were dead, and now You are alive for evermore. Thank You that You hold the keys of death and hell. Lay Your hand on me today and calm my fears. I trust You with my life and my future. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy112' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 8, Paul talks about the Holy Spirit living in believers. He contrasts life in the flesh, controlled by sinful desires, with life in the Spirit. Then he gives this promise: "But if the Spirit of him that raised up Jesus from the dead dwell in you, he that raised up Christ from the dead shall also quicken your mortal bodies by his Spirit that dwelleth in you."

Think about that. The same Spirit who raised Jesus from the grave lives in every believer.

"Quicken" means make alive. One day, God will raise our mortal bodies at the resurrection. But even now, the Spirit brings life to us: strength to obey, power to resist sin, comfort in sorrow and courage in weakness.

Many Christians feel weak and defeated. They try to live the Christian life in their own strength and quickly run out. But we are not meant to live by our own power. We are meant to depend on the Spirit who lives in us.

This is not about a feeling of power or dramatic experiences, though God may give those sometimes. It is about the quiet, steady work of the Spirit, bringing resurrection life into ordinary days.

Today: before a task you feel weak for, pause and pray: "Holy Spirit, the same power that raised Jesus lives in me. Give life to my weakness now."',
  declaration = 'The Spirit who raised Jesus from the dead lives in me. He brings life to my mortal body and strength to my weakness.',
  prayer = 'Holy Spirit, thank You that You live in me, the same Spirit who raised Jesus from the dead. Where I am weak, give me life. Help me depend on Your power and not my own strength today. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy113' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Ephesians 2 starts with a dark picture: "you... were dead in trespasses and sins." Not sick, not weak, but dead. Dead people cannot help themselves.

Then come two of the most hopeful words in the Bible: "But God". "But God, who is rich in mercy, for his great love wherewith he loved us, Even when we were dead in sins, hath quickened us together with Christ, (by grace ye are saved;)"

God is "rich in mercy". Some people are rich in money, land or influence. God is rich in mercy. He has more mercy than we have sin.

He acted "for his great love", not because of anything good in us. He loved us "even when we were dead in sins".

And He "quickened us together with Christ": made us alive with Him. Our new life is connected to Christ''s resurrection.

Paul cannot help adding in brackets: "by grace ye are saved". He wants to make sure we never forget it.

Many people have a "but God" story: "I was addicted, but God...", "I was on the wrong path, but God...", "I had given up, but God..." If you are in Christ, you have one too.

Today: write down your own "but God" story in a few sentences. Thank Him for it, and ask Him for an opportunity to share it with someone this week.',
  declaration = 'I was dead in sin, but God, rich in mercy and great in love, made me alive with Christ. By grace I am saved.',
  prayer = 'Father, thank You for the "but God" in my story. I was lost, but You loved me and made me alive with Christ. Thank You that You are rich in mercy. Help me share my story with someone who needs hope. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy114' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Jesus spoke these words after healing a man who was born blind. The religious leaders, who should have cared for the people like shepherds, threw the man out. Jesus then described Himself as the Good Shepherd who knows His sheep and lays down His life for them.

He compared Himself with a thief: "The thief cometh not, but for to steal, and to kill, and to destroy: I am come that they might have life, and that they might have it more abundantly."

There are two very different kinds of influence on our lives. One steals, kills and destroys: peace, joy, relationships, purpose, faith. The other gives life in abundance.

"More abundantly" does not mean a life of luxury or constant ease. It means a full life: rich in God''s presence, love, purpose, peace and hope, even when circumstances are hard.

The thief can come in many forms: sin, lies about who we are, false teachers who use people, addictions, bitterness, fear. They promise satisfaction but leave destruction.

Jesus is the Good Shepherd. He gives, protects and leads. He laid down His life so that we could live.

Today: think about what is stealing life from you: a habit, a relationship, a way of thinking or a lie you believe. Bring it to Jesus and ask Him to guard you and lead you into abundant life.',
  declaration = 'Jesus came that I might have life, and have it more abundantly. I follow the Good Shepherd, not the thief.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You are my Good Shepherd. Protect me from everything that comes to steal, kill and destroy. Lead me into the full, abundant life You died to give me. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy115' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Isaiah 1 opens with God''s charge against His people. They were offering many sacrifices and keeping religious feasts, but their hands were full of injustice. God said He was tired of their empty worship. He called them to stop doing evil, learn to do good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow.

Then comes this invitation: "Come now, and let us reason together, saith the LORD: though your sins be as scarlet, they shall be as white as snow; though they be red like crimson, they shall be as wool."

Scarlet and crimson were deep red dyes that were very hard to remove from cloth. Sins that seem permanent, stains we think will never come out, God can make white as snow.

Notice that God says, "Come now, and let us reason together." He invites honest conversation. He is not a cruel judge who refuses to listen. He wants us to come to Him, to talk about our sin openly and to receive His cleansing.

The next verses show it is not cheap. God calls for willing obedience. Cleansing comes with a changed heart.

Today: bring the stain you think is too deep to God. Say to Him: "Lord, this sin is scarlet. Make me white as snow." Then ask Him what change He wants you to make in how you treat others.',
  declaration = 'Though my sins were as scarlet, God makes me white as snow. I come and reason with Him, and He cleanses me.',
  prayer = 'Lord, I come to You honestly. You know my scarlet stains. Thank You that You can make me white as snow through Jesus. Cleanse me, and help me live justly and kindly, especially toward the weak. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy116' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'In Romans 6, Paul explains that when Christ died, He died to sin once for all, and now He lives to God. Then he applies it to us: "Likewise reckon ye also yourselves to be dead indeed unto sin, but alive unto God through Jesus Christ our Lord."

"Reckon" is an accounting word. It means to count, to calculate, to consider something as true. Paul is saying: count it as true that you are dead to sin and alive to God.

This is not pretending. It is believing what God says about us. In Christ, our old self was crucified. Sin no longer has the right to command us. We are alive to God, able to respond to Him.

Think of someone who has been released from prison but still behaves like a prisoner: asking permission to eat, waiting at locked doors that are actually open. The doors are open; they need to believe it and walk out.

Feelings may lag behind truth. You may still feel sin''s pull. But you can choose to reckon: "That is not who I am anymore."

The next verses say: do not let sin reign; do not offer your body to it; offer yourselves to God.

Today: when temptation comes, answer it with this truth: "I am dead to sin and alive to God in Christ." Then choose an action that expresses your new life.',
  declaration = 'I reckon myself dead to sin and alive to God through Jesus Christ my Lord.',
  prayer = 'Lord, help me count as true what You say about me. I am dead to sin and alive to You in Christ. When temptation comes, help me remember who I am and choose to live for You. In Jesus'' name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy117' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'John wrote to believers who were surrounded by false teachers and a hostile world. He reminded them who they were: "For whatsoever is born of God overcometh the world: and this is the victory that overcometh the world, even our faith."

"The world" here is not the planet or the people in it. It is the system that opposes God: its values, pressures and temptations. John earlier described it as "the lust of the flesh, and the lust of the eyes, and the pride of life."

The world tells us that money is security, success is identity, pleasure is happiness and God is optional. It pressures us to compromise, to fit in and to live for ourselves.

But John says those who are born of God overcome the world. Not because they are stronger in themselves, but because of faith: trust in Jesus, the Son of God, who has already overcome.

Faith is the victory because it connects us to the Victor. Jesus said, "be of good cheer; I have overcome the world."

Overcoming the world may look like refusing a bribe, staying faithful in marriage, speaking the truth when it costs you, or keeping joy and peace when the world around you is anxious.

Today: identify one way the world is pressuring you. Face it with faith: "Jesus has overcome. By faith in Him, I overcome too."',
  declaration = 'I am born of God, and I overcome the world. My faith in Jesus is the victory.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, You have overcome the world. I am born of God, and I belong to You. Strengthen my faith so that I do not bow to the world''s pressures. Let my life show Your victory. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy118' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Hebrews 2 explains why the Son of God became a man. "Forasmuch then as the children are partakers of flesh and blood, he also himself likewise took part of the same; that through death he might destroy him that had the power of death, that is, the devil; And deliver them who through fear of death were all their lifetime subject to bondage."

Jesus did not save us from a distance. He took on flesh and blood: a real human body that could feel pain, hunger and tiredness, and could die.

And through His death, He destroyed the power of the one who used death to hold people in fear.

Many people live in bondage to the fear of death. It affects how they make decisions, how they treat others, how they cling to money and control. Some live in fear of witchcraft, curses or spiritual attack.

Jesus came to deliver us from that fear. Not by pretending death is not real, but by going through it and coming out victorious. The devil''s power over death is broken for those who belong to Christ.

The next verses say Jesus became like us so He could be a merciful and faithful High Priest, able to help us when we are tempted.

Today: name any fear that has held you in bondage. Declare: "Jesus shared my flesh and blood, died and rose again. I am free from the fear of death."',
  declaration = 'Jesus shared my flesh and blood and destroyed the power of the devil through His death. I am free from the fear of death.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, thank You for becoming one of us and dying to destroy the devil''s power. Set me free from every fear that holds me in bondage. Help me live with courage, knowing You have overcome death. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy119' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;

UPDATE devotions SET reflection = 'Paul wrote this from prison, after many years of serving Christ. He had planted churches, written letters and seen miracles. Yet his greatest desire was still this: "That I may know him, and the power of his resurrection, and the fellowship of his sufferings, being made conformable unto his death."

After all those years, Paul still wanted to know Jesus more. Not just to know about Him, but to know Him personally and deeply.

He wanted to know "the power of his resurrection": life, strength and victory. That is the part we all want.

But he also wanted "the fellowship of his sufferings". Paul understood that knowing Jesus includes sharing in His sufferings: rejection for His sake, sacrifice, carrying our cross. Through suffering, we come to know Him in a way that comfort alone cannot teach.

"Being made conformable unto his death" means being shaped into the pattern of His humble, obedient, self-giving life.

As April ends, after weeks of reflecting on the cross and resurrection, this verse brings it all together. The goal is not religion, success or even blessing. The goal is Jesus Himself.

Today: pray Paul''s prayer slowly: "That I may know Him." Tell God honestly what you want, including what you fear about suffering. Ask Him to make knowing Jesus your highest goal.',
  declaration = 'My highest goal is to know Christ: the power of His resurrection and the fellowship of His sufferings.',
  prayer = 'Lord Jesus, I want to know You, not just know about You. Let me experience the power of Your resurrection. Give me grace to share in Your sufferings when they come, and shape my life to look like Yours. In Your name, Amen.'
WHERE id = 'dy120' AND instr(reflection, char(10)) = 0;
